Input: {"question": "When was Cove Fort built?", "source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00", "start": 0, "end": 82, "text": "Cove Fort Cove Fort is a fort and historical site located in Millard County, Utah."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00", "start": 83, "end": 160, "text": "It was founded in 1867 by Ira Hinckley (the paternal grandfather of Gordon B."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00", "start": 161, "end": 203, "text": "Hinckley) at the request of Brigham Young."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00", "start": 204, "end": 363, "text": "One of its distinctive features is the use of volcanic rock in the construction of the walls, rather than the wood used in many mid-19th-century western forts."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00", "start": 364, "end": 469, "text": "This difference in construction is the reason it is one of very few forts of this period still surviving."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00", "start": 470, "end": 571, "text": "Cove Fort is the closest named place to the western terminus of Interstate 70, resulting in Cove Fort"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01", "start": 0, "end": 92, "text": "families who lived there and about their efforts to provide for the needs of the travelers ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01", "start": 93, "end": 186, "text": "Ira Hinckley , his brother Arza , and local workmen built the fort of volcanic rock in 1867 ."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01", "start": 187, "end": 320, "text": "They built it to protect themselves and travelers from possible Native American raids resulting from the Black Hawk War ( 1865–68 ) ."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01", "start": 321, "end": 363, "text": "Native Americans never attacked the fort ."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01", "start": 364, "end": 500, "text": "During the fort ’ s use as a way station , the Hinckley family maintained good relationships with local Pahvant Ute and Navajo Indians ."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01", "start": 501, "end": 561, "text": "Operating a self-sufficient fort was largely a family affair"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d02", "start": 0, "end": 148, "text": "What to Expect When You Visit the Cove Fort Historic Site What to Expect When You Visit the Cove Fort Historic Site Cove Fort is now open for tours."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d02", "start": 149, "end": 301, "text": "COVID 19 visitor information Updates Cove Fort Historic Site welcomes visitors year-round and is located approximately 30 miles south of Fillmore, Utah."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d02", "start": 302, "end": 443, "text": "The rock fort was built in 1857 and protected telegraph lines and travelers on the road between Salt Lake City and settlements further south."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d02", "start": 444, "end": 606, "text": "Leaders of The Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints called families to manage the fort and ranch, and these families lived and worked there for many years."}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03", "start": 0, "end": 91, "text": "Families who lived there and about their efforts to provide for the needs of the travelers."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03", "start": 92, "end": 182, "text": "Ira Hinckley, his brother Arza, and local workmen built the fort of volcanic rock in 1855."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03", "start": 183, "end": 313, "text": "They built it to protect themselves and travelers from possible Native American raids resulting from the Black Hawk War (1865–68)."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03", "start": 314, "end": 355, "text": "Native Americans never attacked the fort."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03", "start": 356, "end": 488, "text": "During the fort’s use as a way station, the Hinckley family maintained good relationships with local Pahvant Ute and Navajo Indians."}, "D4S6": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03", "start": 489, "end": 550, "text": "Operating a self-sufficient fort was largely a family affair."}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d04", "start": 0, "end": 111, "text": "Cove Fort, County Cork Cove Fort is a small bastioned land battery to the east of Cobh in County Cork, Ireland."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d04", "start": 112, "end": 306, "text": "Built as a coastal defence fortification in 1743, on instruction of the then Vice-Admiral of the Coast, it replaced a number of temporary coastal artillery batteries which defended Cork Harbour."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d04", "start": 307, "end": 498, "text": "The seaward fortifications included a demi-bastioned frontage with three tiers of gun emplacements commanding the harbour's main shipping channel and defending the naval yards at Haulbowline."}, "D5S4": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d04", "start": 499, "end": 657, "text": "While the landward walls included musketry flanking-galleries, later 18th century reports criticised the fact that the fort was overlooked by higher ground to"}}, "candidates": [{"candidate_id": "C0", "answer": "1867", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00:c0", "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C1", "answer": "1857", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d02:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C2", "answer": "1855", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C3", "answer": "1743", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d04:c0"]}], "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00", "card_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d00:c0", "entity": "Cove Fort", "scope": "Construction date", "claim": "Cove Fort was founded in 1867.", "answer": "1867", "quote": "It was founded in 1867 by Ira Hinckley (the paternal grandfather of Gordon B.", "original_document_number": 1}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01", "card_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d01:c0", "entity": "Cove Fort", "scope": "Construction date", "claim": "Ira Hinckley, his brother Arza, and local workmen built the fort of volcanic rock in 1867.", "answer": "1867", "quote": "Ira Hinckley , his brother Arza , and local workmen built the fort of volcanic rock in 1867 .", "original_document_number": 2}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d02", "card_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d02:c0", "entity": "Cove Fort", "scope": "Construction date", "claim": "The rock fort was built in 1857.", "answer": "1857", "quote": "The rock fort was built in 1857 and protected telegraph lines and travelers on the road between Salt Lake City and settlements further south.", "original_document_number": 3}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03", "card_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d03:c0", "entity": "Cove Fort", "scope": "Construction date", "claim": "Ira Hinckley, his brother Arza, and local workmen built the fort of volcanic rock in 1855.", "answer": "1855", "quote": "Ira Hinckley, his brother Arza, and local workmen built the fort of volcanic rock in 1855.", "original_document_number": 4}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d04", "card_id": "ramdocs-0241-f38df35ce5a1:d04:c0", "entity": "Cove Fort", "scope": "Construction date", "claim": "Cove Fort was built as a coastal defence fortification in 1743.", "answer": "1743", "quote": "Built as a coastal defence fortification in 1743, on instruction of the then Vice-Admiral of the Coast, it replaced a number of temporary coastal artillery batteries which defended Cork Harbour.", "original_document_number": 5}], "initial_answers": ["1867", "1857", "1855", "1743"]}

